You are completing a risk-accounting repair for a loss analysis that was
otherwise accepted. The prior response derived the losses, hazards, and
security constraints you will see, but its `risk_dispositions` collection is
incomplete or malformed: some supplied risk cards have no entry, some have
duplicated or malformed entries. You return corrected entries for exactly the
selected risk cards.

## Risk accounting

Account for every selected risk with one entry, using one of two
dispositions:

- `cited` — the risk contributed to at least one loss the response declared;
  list the exact loss IDs in `loss_ids` and omit the `reason` field entirely.
- `not_applicable` — the risk produces no grounded loss for this specific
  system; leave `loss_ids` empty and write a one-sentence `reason` stating
  why it does not apply here.

Every `loss_ids` value must be a loss ID from the declared loss registry in
the user message. Do not invent a loss merely to cite a risk; a risk that
produces no grounded loss stays `not_applicable` with its reason.

A declared loss accounts for a card only through a channel inside the
deployed system: its replies and chat, its tool calls, or the backend
records and services those tool calls reach. Reject a declared loss whose
only channel is a third-party model provider, model vendor, or other outside
service, or prompts used as a channel through which data leaks: do not cite
it for any card. A loss that also names a channel inside the system accounts
for a card through that channel alone. Cite another declared loss that
reaches the card's harm through this system's own channels, or, when none
does, mark the card `not_applicable` with a reason that says so.

